\details{
Everything about the run is settled on the user's own machine, before anything
is copied: the arguments are checked as \code{\link[=fit_hazard_models]{fit_hazard_models()}} checks them, the
unit left out in cross-validation is chosen and, with \code{kfold = TRUE}, the units
are assigned to folds once for all the models. A mistake therefore is quickly
found here and not on the cluster hours later.

The bundle holds:
\itemize{
\item \code{settings.sh}, the account, time, memory, cores and modules, written once and
read by every script of the bundle. It is the one file to edit when a task
needs more time or memory.
\item \code{install.sh} and \code{install.R}, which install what the bundle needs into the R of
the cluster: rTFT at the version that wrote the bundle, from \code{rtft_source},
cmdstanr, and CmdStan. It is run once on each cluster, on the login node or, with
\verb{bash install.sh --job}, as a Slurm job.
\item \code{check.sh}, which checks the bundle and the R of the cluster on the login node.
\item \code{submit.sh}, which submits the tasks to Slurm as job arrays, one for the fits
and one for the refits, queued to start when the fits have ended; \code{task.sh},
which Slurm runs for each task and which calls \code{\link[=run_hpc_task]{run_hpc_task()}}; and
\code{status.sh}, which calls \code{\link[=hpc_status]{hpc_status()}} to say what each task has done.
\item \code{README.txt}, the steps to take, with the commands filled in.
\item \verb{bundle/}, the data (\code{data.rds}), the models (\code{models.rds}), the settings of the
run (\code{settings.rds}), the tasks as tables to read (\code{tasks-fit.csv}, and
\code{tasks-kfold.csv} with \code{kfold = TRUE}), and \code{checksums.txt}, the checksum of
each of these files, against which the cluster checks what it received.
\item \verb{stan/}, \verb{results/} and \verb{logs/}, empty, for the compiled models, the results and
what each task prints.
}

The fits on the cluster use the cmdstanr backend, and each task runs its chains on
as many cores as there are chains, so \code{backend} and \code{cores} cannot be given in
\code{...}.
